.52453 11.8926L3.6173 11.6621C3.71685 11.4352 3.83766 11.2176 3.97765 11.0127L4.15343 10.7783C4.34386 10.5462 4.58164 10.312 4.90538 9.98829L11.1964 3.6963L11.3312 3.56837ZM5.84581 10.9287C5.49664 11.2779 5.31252 11.4634 5.18663 11.6162L5.07531 11.7627C4.98188 11.8995 4.90151 12.0448 4.83507 12.1963L4.77355 12.3506C4.73321 12.4607 4.70242 12.5761 4.66808 12.7451L4.54113 13.4619L4.14269 15.8555L4.14171 15.8574H4.14464L6.5382 15.458L7.25499 15.332C7.424 15.2977 7.5394 15.2669 7.64953 15.2266L7.80285 15.165C7.95455 15.0986 8.09947 15.0174 8.23644 14.9238L8.3839 14.8135C8.53668 14.6876 8.72225 14.5035 9.0714 14.1543L14.0587 9.16602L10.8331 5.94044L5.84581 10.9287ZM15.3634 4.63673C14.5281 3.80141 13.2057 3.74938 12.3097 4.48048L12.1368 4.63673L11.7735 5.00001L15.0001 8.22559L15.3634 7.86329L15.5196 7.68946C16.2015 6.85326 16.2015 5.64676 15.5196 4.81056L15.3634 4.63673Z"></path></svg></span></button></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full focus:outline-none scroll-mt-[calc(var(--header-height)+min(200px,max(70px,20svh)))]" tabindex="-1" dir="auto" data-turn-id="b85b5697-cd2e-48fb-9c84-bb72f9156f05" data-testid="conversation-turn-10" data-scroll-anchor="false" data-turn="assistant"><h6 class="sr-only">ChatGPT said:</h6><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] thread-sm:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] thread-lg:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] thread-lg:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="b85b5697-cd2e-48fb-9c84-bb72f9156f05" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="161">That’s an elegant way to put it. Let me phrase it in academic language that situates it alongside both Bayesian inference and RSVP’s field-theoretic commitments:</p>
<hr data-start="163" data-end="166">
<h3 data-start="168" data-end="220">First-Person Comparison as Bayesian Interference</h3>
<p data-start="221" data-end="910">In the RSVP frame, <strong data-start="240" data-end="266">first-person inference</strong> can be understood not as the passive reception of data but as an <strong data-start="332" data-end="377">active comparison of generative manifolds</strong>. Each candidate manifold encodes a possible trajectory of the self-model (Φ, 𝒗, S configuration). Bayesian updating then amounts to computing the <strong data-start="525" data-end="549">interference pattern</strong>—a kind of beat frequency—between manifolds. The degree of constructive or destructive interference reflects the <strong data-start="662" data-end="704">compatibility of generative hypotheses</strong> with incoming evidence. This shifts the emphasis from discrete hypothesis testing to a <strong data-start="792" data-end="824">geometric resonance calculus</strong>, in which coherence across manifolds serves as the measure of posterior plausibility.</p>
<h3 data-start="912" data-end="966">Extending Intentionality Beyond the Markov Blanket</h3>
<p data-start="967" data-end="1679">On this view, what classical cognitive science terms “theory of mind” is not a sui generis module for social cognition but an <strong data-start="1093" data-end="1128">extension of intentional stance</strong> across Markovian boundaries. Within the self, intentionality is the directedness of inference toward maintaining coherence of the self-model. When another agent is encountered, their observable trajectories are treated as evidence of <strong data-start="1363" data-end="1389">animacy and motivation</strong>, i.e., as signatures of another generative manifold entangled with one’s own. To model the other is simply to extend the same <strong data-start="1516" data-end="1548">Bayesian manifold comparison</strong> outward, evaluating compatibility between self and alter manifolds in much the same way as between internal priors and new data.</p>
<h3 data-start="1681" data-end="1707">Recursive Entanglement</h3>
<p data-start="1708" data-end="2279">This extension produces recursive coupling: each agent computes interference not only between its internal hypotheses but also between its own manifold and the inferred manifold of the other. Compatibility (constructive interference) manifests phenomenologically as attunement; incompatibility (destructive interference) as rupture. From this standpoint, “theory of mind” reduces to <strong data-start="2091" data-end="2137">cross-boundary Bayesian manifold alignment</strong>, and what we label affect (positive or negative valence) is the <strong data-start="2202" data-end="2278">felt measure of the beat frequency between overlapping generative fields</strong>.</p>
