pan></span></button></div></div></span><div class="max-w-[calc(0.8*var(--thread-content-max-width,40rem))]"></div></div></div><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="f1f1c349-a229-4047-8e17-6f94c632c5b0" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5-thinking"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="243">Below is a compact formalization you can drop into a technical appendix. It makes the “first-person comparison” precise as <strong data-start="123" data-end="157">Bayesian manifold interference</strong> and treats “theory of mind” as <strong data-start="189" data-end="242">intentionality extended beyond the Markov blanket</strong>.</p>
<h1 data-start="245" data-end="309">Formal sketch: Bayesian interference over generative manifolds</h1>
<h2 data-start="311" data-end="348">Generative manifolds and path laws</h2>
<p data-start="349" data-end="390">Let a <strong data-start="355" data-end="378">generative manifold</strong> be a triple</p>
