The </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{manifold hypothesis} posits that high-dimensional real-world data distributions are supported on smooth, low-dimensional submanifolds of ambient Euclidean space. In the RSVP framework, this amounts to assuming that semantic manifolds </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(X,g,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> arise as intrinsic supports of data with bounded curvature and positive reach, enabling entropy-respecting projections between modalities.

