01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-latex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Discussion: From Manifold Hypothesis to RSVP Generalization}

The manifold hypothesis has been central to explanations of why deep learning 
generalizes despite operating in extremely high-dimensional input spaces 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{Fefferman2016Testing,Chollet2021DeepLearning,Gorban2018Blessing}. 
Its claim that real-world data concentrate on low-dimensional latent structures 
immediately reduces the effective complexity of the learning problem. 
From the RSVP perspective, this empirical observation acquires a precise 
geometric and entropic formulation.

First, Propositions~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{prop:knor-distortion} and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{prop:knor-curvature} 
show that neighborhood overlap (KNOR) directly estimates the connector-induced 
metric distortion </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span> D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and extrapolated curvature </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. 
Hence, the empirical ``collapse of neighborhoods'' documented in large-scale 
multimodal systems corresponds to a measurable curvature shear within the 
RSVP manifold. In effect, KNOR becomes an operational proxy for the stability 
of the semantic geometry.

Second, Corollary~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{cor:entropy-knor} elevates this geometric distortion 
into a thermodynamic statement: KNOR loss lower-bounds connector entropy 
production </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>[F</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>|</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span>]</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. This provides a unifying RSVP interpretation: 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{to the extent that a connector map distorts neighborhoods, it necessarily 
consumes the entropy budget of the manifold}. Irreversibility results 
(Proposition~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{prop:procrustes}) clarify why post-hoc alignment cannot 
restore information once dissipated.

Third, the restricted isometry analysis (Proposition~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{prop:ri-entropy}) 
clarifies the role of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{negentropic corridors}. Smooth generalization arises 
when answer-relevant regions of the manifold preserve local injectivity and 
low divergence. This parallels the ML intuition that interpolation along 
manifolds enables generalization: in RSVP language, interpolation is the 
traversal of entropy-minimizing flows through corridors where 
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>[F</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>|</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span>]</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is controlled.

Finally, the conditional results 
(Proposition~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{prop:conditional-knor}) point toward a refined experimental 
prediction: task-conditioned KNOR should better explain performance variance 
than global KNOR. This suggests a precise RSVP framing of 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{task-aware generalization}: only corridors aligned with the conditional 
context matter for semantic fidelity. Such a claim generalizes the manifold 
hypothesis into a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{contextual manifold hypothesis}—that data lie not only 
on manifolds, but on submanifolds whose geometry is dynamically selected by 
the task context and whose entropy budgets constrain achievable performance.

Taken together, these results extend the manifold hypothesis into the RSVP 
framework. Generalization in deep learning emerges not simply from the presence 
of low-dimensional latent manifolds, but from the preservation of curvature, 
entropy, and sheaf-consistency under connector maps. Failures of generalization 
reflect precisely the breakdown of these geometric and entropic conditions. 
Thus, the extrapolated Riemannian curvature of semantic manifolds provides a 
natural bridge between the statistical insight of the manifold hypothesis and 
a full field-theoretic account of semantic generalization.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
