Beyond the classical manifold hypothesis, several recent contributions extend 
its scope in ways directly relevant to RSVP. Brown et al.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{Brown2023Union} propose the </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Union of Manifolds Hypothesis}, which 
suggests that real-world data may be distributed not on a single smooth 
submanifold but on a collection of overlapping submanifolds, corresponding to 
heterogeneous generative processes. This aligns with RSVP’s sheaf-theoretic 
perspective: semantic data are naturally modeled as local sections that require 
gluing across overlaps, and failures of consistency manifest as cohomological 
obstructions. 

Lee </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{Lee2023AutoencoderGeometry} offers a complementary view by framing 
autoencoders through differential geometry, interpreting encoder–decoder maps 
as approximate isometries between high- and low-dimensional manifolds. This 
perspective dovetails with RSVP’s connector formalism: curvature distortion and 
entropy production provide principled ways of quantifying the quality of 
autoencoder reconstructions, connecting geometric fidelity to informational 
loss.

Together, these works illustrate a broader trend: the manifold hypothesis is 
no longer regarded as a static claim about low-dimensional embeddings but as a 
dynamic principle encompassing unions, overlaps, and nonlinear reconstructions. 
RSVP generalizes and unifies these directions by supplying the field-theoretic, 
functorial, and entropy-based scaffold needed to evaluate and compare manifold 
mappings across contexts.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
<hr data-start="1798" data-end="1801">
<p data-start="1803" data-end="1859">This slots naturally after the <strong data-start="1834" data-end="1848">Discussion</strong> section.</p>
<p data-start="1861" data-end="2144" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Do you want me to also weave in the <strong data-start="1897" data-end="1927">information geometry angle</strong> (Fisher metric, Caticha 2015; Kirchhoff et al. 2018) here, so the “Further Reading” points not just to extensions of the manifold hypothesis but also to its connections with information geometry and active inference?</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.29688 7.47559L4.16699 7.53125C3.86488 7.68518 3.61186 7.