Our proposal of extrapolated Riemannian curvature for semantic manifolds draws 
on and extends several established traditions in geometry, information theory, 
and category-theoretic semantics.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{The manifold hypothesis.}
The manifold hypothesis </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{Fefferman2016Testing,Gorban2018Blessing,Chollet2021DeepLearning} 
posits that high-dimensional data concentrate on low-dimensional smooth 
submanifolds of ambient Euclidean space. This principle underlies the success of 
nonlinear dimensionality reduction and deep learning. Within RSVP, the manifold 
hypothesis appears as the claim that semantic data are always carried on 
entropy-constrained manifolds </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(X,g,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, and that connector maps must 
preserve curvature and entropy budgets to enable generalization. Extensions such 
as the Union of Manifolds Hypothesis </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{Brown2023Union} and geometric analyses 
of autoencoders </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{Lee2023AutoencoderGeometry} reinforce the need for 
sheaf-theoretic gluing and curvature-aware reconstruction.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Information geometry and active inference.}
Information geometry provides the canonical Fisher metric on statistical 
manifolds </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{Caticha2015Geometry}. This has been applied in neuroscience 
through predictive coding and the free energy principle, with Markov blankets 
demarcating generative manifolds </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{Kirchhoff2018MarkovBlankets}. RSVP 
generalizes this program: the extrapolated curvature </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and connector 
entropy </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>[F</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>|</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span>]</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> extend Fisher geometry to field-theoretic settings 
where curvature distortion and entropy production can be tracked under 
projection.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Categorical and sheaf-theoretic methods.}
Sheaf theory and categorical logic provide the natural framework for modeling 
local-to-global consistency. Failures of patch-wise reconstruction can be 
expressed as cohomological obstructions, and connector maps are best treated as 
functors between categories of fields. This connects to broader traditions in 
categorical probability </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{jacobs2015new,fritz2020synthetic} and 
topos-theoretic semantics </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{lawvere1970quantifiers,street1972two}, 
positioning RSVP as an enriched categorical framework where entropy is tracked 
as a functorial cost.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Geometric curvature in networks.}
Discrete analogues such as Forman–Ricci curvature </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{forman2003bochner} have 
been used to detect robustness, phase transitions, and topological 
reconfiguration in complex networks. Our use of extrapolated curvature follows 
this line, but grounds it in a field-theoretic interpretation: curvature shifts 
in connector maps correspond to semantic entropy production, not just 
combinatorial irregularity.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Summary.}
In summary, this work situates itself at the intersection of four traditions: 
the manifold hypothesis and its extensions, information geometry and active 
inference, categorical and sheaf-theoretic semantics, and discrete curvature in 
network theory. RSVP’s novelty lies in synthesizing these into a single 
framework where manifold structure, curvature, and entropy budgets jointly 
govern the fidelity of semantic projection.

