01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-latex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Manifold Semantics and Diagrammatic Adequacy}

The manifold hypothesis suggests that high-dimensional structures of meaning
and data can be understood as lying on comparatively low-dimensional
submanifolds. In SPC, the operational and denotational semantics both induce
such manifolds: the operational semantics traces reduction paths through a
syntactic manifold of terms, while the categorical semantics interprets these
reductions in a semantic manifold structured by presheaves, tensors, and
probability distributions.

Adequacy of the semantics then requires that these two manifolds be related by
a smooth functor: every operational trajectory corresponds to a path in the
semantic manifold, and vice versa. This relationship is captured formally by a
family of commutative diagrams. Each diagram ensures that operational reduction
and categorical interpretation are functorially aligned, respecting both local
structure (patchwise reductions) and global curvature (the coherence of the
semantic category).

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Commutative Diagrams (Adequacy Proofs)}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Sphere/Pop.}
Abstraction and application correspond to exponential structure on the semantic
manifold. Adequacy requires that operational </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\beta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-reduction agrees with
categorical evaluation:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\resizebox</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textwidth</span></span><span>}{!}{
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{tikzcd}[column sep=small]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Gamma</span></span><span>} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{A}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[r,"{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\langle</span></span><span> f , </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rangle</span></span><span>}"]
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[d,swap,"{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{oper.}}"]
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{A </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Rightarrow</span></span><span> B} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{A}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[r,"</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{ev}"]
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{B} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Gamma</span></span><span>} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{A}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[rr,swap,"{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{Pop}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{Sphere}(x{:}A.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>t),u)}}"]
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{B}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{tikzcd}
}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Merge.}
Parallel composition corresponds to the tensor product of the manifold’s
monoidal structure:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\resizebox</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textwidth</span></span><span>}{!}{
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{tikzcd}[column sep=small]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{t} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{u}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[rr,"</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cong</span></span><span>"]
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[d,swap,"{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\shortstack</span></span><span>{oper.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>flattening}}"]
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{t} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{u}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[d,"{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{tensor}}"] </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{Merge}(t,u)}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[rr,equal]
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{Merge}(t,u)}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{tikzcd}
}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Choice.}
Probabilistic branching corresponds to convex combination within the
distribution monad, respecting the statistical manifold’s Fisher geometry:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\resizebox</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textwidth</span></span><span>}{!}{
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{tikzcd}[column sep=small]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Gamma</span></span><span>}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[r,"{(p,t,u)}"]
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[d,swap,"{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{sampling}}"]
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> [0,1] </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[r,"</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{mix}"]
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Dist}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{A}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Gamma</span></span><span>}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[rr,swap,"{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{Choice}(p,t,u)}}"]
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Dist}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{A})
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{tikzcd}
}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Independent Channels.}
Aggregated risks correspond to product measures across independent
submanifolds, demonstrating curvature additivity under tensoring:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\resizebox</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textwidth</span></span><span>}{!}{
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{tikzcd}[column sep=small]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\prod</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Dist}(O)
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[r,"</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span>"]
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[d,swap,"{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{oper. Merge}}"]
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Dist}(O</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>n)
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[r,"{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{anyDoom}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\*</span></span><span>}"]
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Dist}(O) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Dist}(O)
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[rr,equal]
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Dist}(O)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{tikzcd}
}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Summary.}
Taken together, these diagrams ensure that SPC’s operational manifold of terms
and its categorical manifold of denotations are functorially equivalent. In this
sense, SPC realises the manifold hypothesis internally: both syntax and
semantics inhabit structured manifolds, and adequacy guarantees that their
geometric and probabilistic curvature is preserved under reduction.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
