01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-latex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Manifold Semantics, Sheaf Gluing, and Adequacy}

The manifold hypothesis suggests that high-dimensional data and meaning lie on
low-dimensional structured submanifolds. In SPC, operational reduction and
categorical interpretation can be viewed as distinct but coupled manifolds:
the syntactic manifold of terms and reductions, and the semantic manifold of
denotations in a categorical topos enriched with probability and tensor
structure.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Local Patches and Sheaf Structure}

Every term </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> inhabits a local patch of this semantic manifold, determined by
its typing context </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and type </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. The categorical semantics interprets
each patch via a functor:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\colon</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Syn} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Sem},
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
mapping syntactic derivations to semantic objects. Adequacy demands that these
local interpretations glue coherently into a global structure. Formally, the
collection </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> U </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{U </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{U}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> for a cover
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{U}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> of the syntactic manifold forms a presheaf; adequacy asserts that
this presheaf is in fact a sheaf, satisfying the gluing condition:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span> s</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> 
s</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i|</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cap</span></span><span> U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j} = s</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j|</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cap</span></span><span> U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Rightarrow</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\exists</span></span><span> !</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> s </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\bigcup</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Thus, operational and denotational perspectives agree both locally (on overlaps
of reductions) and globally (on composite programs).

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Diagrammatic Adequacy as Sheaf Gluing}

The commutative diagrams of SPC are not merely proof artifacts: they realize
the sheaf condition diagrammatically. Each adequacy diagram witnesses that
local operational reductions commute with global semantic interpretations. For
instance:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Sphere/Pop.}
Abstraction and application correspond to exponential patches of the semantic
manifold. Adequacy guarantees that the gluing of these patches coincides with
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\beta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-reduction:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{tikzcd}[column sep=small]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Gamma</span></span><span>} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{A}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[r,"{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\langle</span></span><span> f , </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rangle</span></span><span>}"]
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[d,swap,"{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{oper.}}"]
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{A </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Rightarrow</span></span><span> B} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{A}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[r,"</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{ev}"]
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{B} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Gamma</span></span><span>} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{A}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[rr,swap,"{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{Pop}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{Sphere}(x{:}A.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>t),u)}}"]
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{B}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{tikzcd}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Merge.}
Concurrency corresponds to tensor products of local patches; adequacy ensures
that operational flattening and categorical tensor yield the same glued
section:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{tikzcd}[column sep=small]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{t} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{u}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[rr,"</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cong</span></span><span>"]
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[d,swap,"{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\shortstack</span></span><span>{oper.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>flattening}}"]
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{t} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{u}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[d,"{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{tensor}}"] </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{Merge}(t,u)}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[rr,equal]
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{Merge}(t,u)}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{tikzcd}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Choice.}
Probabilistic choice corresponds to convex mixtures in the distribution sheaf.
Here, adequacy expresses bilinearity of gluing measures:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{tikzcd}[column sep=small]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Gamma</span></span><span>}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[r,"{(p,t,u)}"]
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[d,swap,"{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{sampling}}"]
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> [0,1] </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[r,"</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{mix}"]
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Dist}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{A}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Gamma</span></span><span>}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[rr,swap,"{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{Choice}(p,t,u)}}"]
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Dist}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sem</span></span><span>{A})
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{tikzcd}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Independent Channels.}
For risk aggregation, the sheaf condition ensures that independent local
channels glue into a coherent global distribution:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{tikzcd}[column sep=small]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\prod</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Dist}(O)
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[r,"</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\otimes</span></span><span>"]
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[d,swap,"{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{oper. Merge}}"]
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Dist}(O</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>n)
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[r,"{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{anyDoom}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\*</span></span><span>}"]
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Dist}(O) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Dist}(O)
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[rr,equal]
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Dist}(O)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{tikzcd}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Global Curvature and Semantic Manifolds}

In geometric terms, adequacy ensures that the semantic manifold is curvature
preserving with respect to operational reductions: no distortion arises when
passing from syntax to semantics. Failures of adequacy would manifest as
``entropy tears'' in the sheaf, where local reductions cannot be consistently
glued. By proving adequacy, SPC internalizes the manifold hypothesis: syntax
and semantics both live on structured manifolds, and the sheaf condition
ensures their gluing is smooth, faithful, and globally coherent.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
