From the perspective of information geometry, the semantic manifold carries a
distinguished Riemannian structure given by the Fisher information metric
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. This metric measures the sensitivity of probability distributions to
changes in parameters and provides the canonical geometry for statistical
manifolds. In the context of SPC, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Dist}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> A </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is
not just a set of distributions but a Fisher manifold </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}, g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.

Adequacy of the semantics can therefore be strengthened: not only must the
operational and categorical views commute diagrammatically, they must also
preserve the Fisher information. That is, for every reduction </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> t'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, the
semantic interpretation must satisfy
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cong</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> t' </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
up to isometry on the statistical manifold. Failures of adequacy would manifest
as distortions in the information metric, measurable as excess entropy
production or loss of local injectivity.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Sheaf-theoretic perspective.}
Each local patch </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> of the syntactic manifold is interpreted as a statistical
patch </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> U </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span>, g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. The sheaf condition ensures that local
Fisher metrics glue into a consistent global metric. Formally, if </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span> U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>
is an open cover and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span> g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{F,i} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> are the induced Fisher metrics on
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, then adequacy requires that
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{F,i}|</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cap</span></span><span> U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j} = g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{F,j}|</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cap</span></span><span> U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j},
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
so that the global Fisher metric </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is well defined across the manifold.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Curvature preservation.}
Beyond metric equivalence, adequacy requires preservation of curvature
invariants. The Fisher metric induces a Riemannian curvature tensor </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>;
commutative adequacy diagrams can be interpreted as guarantees that
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span>) = R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> t' </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
ensuring that operational reductions do not introduce geometric shear or warp
in the statistical structure. This aligns with the manifold hypothesis: both
syntax and semantics reside in curved spaces, and adequacy proves that their
curvatures coincide under interpretation.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Summary.}
Incorporating information geometry deepens the manifold reading of SPC. The
commutative diagrams guarantee semantic adequacy locally, the sheaf condition
guarantees global coherence, and Fisher-metric preservation guarantees
information-theoretic integrity. In this sense, SPC operationalizes the
manifold hypothesis by ensuring that syntax, semantics, and statistical
geometry all align under reduction, gluing, and curvature preservation.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
<hr data-start="2947" data-end="2950">
<p data-start="2952" data-end="2990">This way, the section ties together:</p>
<ul data-start="2991" data-end="3152">
<li data-start="2991" data-end="3031">
<p data-start="2993" data-end="3031"><strong data-start="2993" data-end="3006">Manifolds</strong> (syntax vs semantics).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3032" data-end="3081">
<p data-start="3034" data-end="3081"><strong data-start="3034" data-end="3045">Sheaves</strong> (local diagrams → global gluing).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3082" data-end="3152">
<p data-start="3084" data-end="3152"><strong data-start="3084" data-end="3108">Information geometry</strong> (Fisher metric &amp; curvature preservation).</p>
</li>
</ul>
