where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Syn}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is the syntactic category (terms, types, reductions) and
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Sem}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is the semantic category (objects as manifolds, morphisms as
maps between them). To incorporate information geometry, we enrich
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Sem}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> with Fisher metrics, writing </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{StatMan}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> for the
category of statistical manifolds and information-preserving maps.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Information-preserving functor.}
Adequacy requires that </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> factor through
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{StatMan}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\colon</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Syn} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xrightarrow</span></span><span>{F} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{StatMan}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xrightarrow</span></span><span>{U} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Sem},
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a functor that assigns to each syntactic object </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Gamma</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vdash</span></span><span> t:A)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>
a statistical manifold </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span>, g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is the
forgetful functor that discards the Fisher structure. 

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Natural transformations.}
Each operational reduction </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>r: t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> t'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> induces a commuting square
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{tikzcd}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[r,"</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> r </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span>"] </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[d,"g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F"'] </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> t' </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[d,"g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F"] </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span>, g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[r,"</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cong</span></span><span>"'] </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> t' </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span>, g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{tikzcd}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
expressing that the reduction map preserves the Fisher metric. In categorical
terms, this is a naturality condition: for every reduction morphism </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, the
family </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span> g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> defines a natural transformation
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\colon</span></span><span> F </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Rightarrow</span></span><span> F,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
ensuring metric invariance along reductions.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Sheaf gluing as descent.}
From the sheaf perspective, the statistical manifold semantics is a stack:
sections over patches glue not only at the level of sets, but also at the level
of enriched Fisher metrics. The descent condition states that local isometries
on overlaps assemble into a global isometry. Formally, for an open cover
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{U}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> of the syntactic manifold,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\check</span></span><span>{H}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>1(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{U}, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{Iso}(g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F)) = 0,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
so that metric-preserving local data glue uniquely to a global Fisher structure.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Curvature as a natural invariant.}
Finally, the Riemann curvature tensor </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> can be regarded as a natural
invariant of the functor </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Adequacy requires that </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be preserved up to
natural isomorphism:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cong</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
This expresses curvature preservation categorically: the semantic functor
commutes with curvature evaluation, ensuring that operational reductions do not
alter the information-geometric invariants.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Summary.}
In this enriched view, adequacy is the statement that </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>
is an information-preserving functor into </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{StatMan}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, equipped with
natural transformations that preserve Fisher metrics, curvature invariants, and
sheaf-theoretic gluing. This unifies manifold semantics, sheaf theory, and
information geometry into a categorical adequacy theorem for SPC.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
<hr data-start="3343" data-end="3346">
<p data-start="3348" data-end="3396">This ties everything into one categorical frame:</p>
<ul data-start="3398" data-end="3655">
<li data-start="3398" data-end="3464">
<p data-start="3400" data-end="3464"><strong data-start="3400" data-end="3424">Functorial semantics</strong>: $\mathsf{Syn} \to \mathsf{StatMan}$.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3465" data-end="3535">
<p data-start="3467" data-end="3535"><strong data-start="3467" data-end="3494">Natural transformations</strong>: metric preservation along reductions.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3536" data-end="3604">
<p data-start="3538" data-end="3604"><strong data-start="3538" data-end="3565">Sheaf gluing as descent</strong>: overlaps preserve Fisher structure.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3605" data-end="3655">
<p data-start="3607" data-end="3655"><strong data-start="3607" data-end="3631">Curvature invariance</strong>: naturality of $R_F$.</p>
</li>
</ul>
