We now consolidate the manifold, sheaf, and information-geometric perspectives
into a single formal adequacy result. Intuitively, the theorem states that SPC’s
categorical semantics not only commutes with operational reduction, but also
preserves the statistical manifold structure, including the Fisher metric and
curvature invariants.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[SPC Adequacy Theorem]
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Syn}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be the syntactic category of SPC terms and reductions, and
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{StatMan}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> the category of statistical manifolds equipped with Fisher
metrics </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and curvature tensors </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Then the interpretation functor
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\colon</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Syn} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{StatMan}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
satisfies the following properties:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{enumerate}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Commutativity (Diagrammatic Adequacy).}  
  For every operational reduction </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> t'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, the square
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{tikzcd}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[r,"</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> r </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span>"] </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[d,"g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F"'] </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span>
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> t' </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[d,"g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F"] </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
  (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span>, g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ar</span></span><span>[r,"</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cong</span></span><span>"'] </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> t' </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span>, g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F)
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{tikzcd}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
  commutes, showing that operational semantics and denotational semantics agree
  pointwise.

  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Sheaf Condition (Global Coherence).}  
  The family of local interpretations </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> over an
  open cover </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{U}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> of the syntactic manifold forms a sheaf: local
  sections that agree on overlaps glue uniquely to a global section. This
  ensures coherence of syntax-to-semantics interpretation at the manifold level.

  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Metric Preservation (Information Geometry).}  
  The functor </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is enriched over
  </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{StatMan}, g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, meaning it preserves Fisher information up to
  isometry:
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
  g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span>) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cong</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> t' </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span>)
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{whenever } t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> t'.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Curvature Invariance.}  
  The curvature tensor </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a natural invariant: for all reductions </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>r</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>,
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
  R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span>) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cong</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>F(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\llbracket</span></span><span> t' </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rrbracket</span></span><span>).
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
  Thus reductions preserve the global geometric invariants of the statistical
  manifold.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{enumerate}

Together, these properties establish that SPC is </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{adequate} with respect to
its categorical semantics: operational reduction is faithfully represented as a
Fisher-preserving, curvature-preserving sheaf functor into </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{StatMan}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Corollary (Entropy Tears).}
Failure of adequacy manifests as a violation of one of the four properties
above: non-commuting diagrams, non-gluable local sections, metric distortion, or
curvature mismatch. Such failures correspond to ``entropy tears'' in the
semantic manifold, where operational and denotational perspectives no longer
align smoothly.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
<hr data-start="3176" data-end="3179">
<p data-start="3181" data-end="3246">This theorem encapsulates the whole section. It tells the reader:</p>
<ul data-start="3248" data-end="3478">
<li data-start="3248" data-end="3326">
<p data-start="3250" data-end="3326">SPC semantics is a <strong data-start="3269" data-end="3280">functor</strong> into the category of statistical manifolds.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3327" data-end="3426">
<p data-start="3329" data-end="3426">Adequacy = commutativity + sheaf coherence + Fisher metric preservation + curvature invariance.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3427" data-end="3478">
<p data-start="3429" data-end="3478">Failures = entropy tears (nice RSVP resonance).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="3480" data-end="3716" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Do you want me to <strong data-start="3498" data-end="3537">push it further into a proof sketch</strong> (e.g., outline how one shows each condition holds, referencing the adequacy diagrams, sheaf descent, and Fisher-metric invariance)? That would make it a publishable-style result.</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.29688 7.47559L4.16699 7.53125C3.86488 7.68518 3.61186 7.9196 3.43555 8.20703L3.36524 8.33399C3.28478 8.