01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-latex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Related Directions: Geometric Neuroscience and Interbrain Networks}

Recent work in social neuroscience has independently converged on the use of
geometric structures to analyze dynamic neural interactions. Hinrichs, Guzmán,
and Weber </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{hinrichs2025geometry} propose a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{geometry of interbrain
networks} that replaces correlation-based synchrony metrics with discrete
curvature measures on hyperscanning graphs. Their framework closely parallels
the manifold- and sheaf-theoretic adequacy conditions we have outlined for SPC.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Discrete Curvature in Interbrain Networks}

Interbrain networks are constructed by linking neural regions across two (or
more) interacting agents, with edges weighted by inter-brain coupling measures
such as PLV. Traditional correlation-based synchrony measures (cf.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{hakim2023quantification,hamilton2021hyperscanning}) offer descriptive but
limited insights into the mechanisms of interaction. By contrast, discrete
geometry—specifically, Forman–Ricci curvature (FRC) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{forman2003bochner} and
Ollivier–Ricci curvature (ORC) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{ollivier2009ricci}—characterizes network
expansion, contraction, and routing dynamics.

For example, FRC of an edge </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>e=(i,j)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> in a weighted network is defined as
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
F(e) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span>=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> w</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>e </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>(
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{z</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i}{w</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>e} + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{z</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j}{w</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>e}
- </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sim</span></span><span> i,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\neq</span></span><span> e}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{z</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sqrt</span></span><span>{w</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>e w</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i}}}
- </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sim</span></span><span> j,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\neq</span></span><span> e}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{z</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sqrt</span></span><span>{w</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>e w</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j}}}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>z</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i,z</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> are node weights and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>w</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> the edge weight. Positive curvature
indicates redundancy within a densely connected region; negative curvature
highlights bridge-like connections between modules. ORC, in turn, interprets
curvature via Wasserstein distances on neighborhood measures, capturing
information-routing biases between diffusion and shortest-path traversal.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Entropy of Curvature Distributions}

Building on these notions, Hinrichs et al. introduce the entropy of the FRC
distribution as a detector of phase transitions in interbrain dynamics:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{RC}(G</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t) = - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span> f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{RC}(x) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\log</span></span><span> f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{RC}(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>dx,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{RC}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is the empirical density of curvature values across the network
at time </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Divergences in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{RC}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> indicate qualitative shifts in network
geometry—rupture, repair, or reorganization—which correspond to behavioral
transitions such as cooperation, conflict, or re-attunement. This methodology
extends earlier geometric brain-network analyses
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{weber2019curvature,chatterjee2021detecting} and resonates with the
rupture–repair cycles modeled in recursive affective inference
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{hinrichs2025hyperscanning}.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Connections to SPC and RSVP}

From our perspective, curvature-entropy divergences in interbrain graphs play
the same role as adequacy diagrams in SPC or entropy tears in RSVP:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> In SPC, adequacy ensures commutative diagrams glue into a global sheaf
  without distortion. Failures mark entropy tears in the semantic manifold.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> In RSVP, redshift and cognitive misattunement are modeled as curvature
  reconfigurations of scalar–vector–entropy fields.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> In geometric hyperscanning, entropy peaks in FRC distributions mark
  rupture and reorganization of the shared interbrain manifold.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

All three frameworks converge on the same mathematical language: curvature as a
geometric signal of structural change, entropy of curvature distributions as a
phase-transition detector, and sheaf gluing as the condition of global
coherence. This triangulation suggests a common research program: to model
syntactic, semantic, and neural manifolds within a unified information-geometric
framework, where adequacy, synchrony, and attunement are all curvature-preserving
gluing conditions.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Towards Interpersonalized Geometry}

This synthesis also aligns with the broader call for principled models of brain
network complexity </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{kulkarni2024principles} and with Sporns’ emphasis on
meso-scale features in social neuroscience </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{sporns2010networks}. By
recasting hyperscanning as a geometric–categorical analysis, we can regard
interacting brains as forming a joint sheaf over time-varying patches, with
curvature dynamics guiding the gluing. Failures of gluing correspond to social
rupture; successful descent corresponds to repair and attunement.

In this sense, the SPC Adequacy Theorem and the discrete-geometry methods for
interbrain networks are two facets of the same project: proving that coherence
in social, semantic, and computational manifolds is nothing other than the
preservation of curvature and Fisher information across levels of description.
