01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-latex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Curvature, Entropy, and Interbrain Geometry}

A complementary strand of research in social neuroscience has recently begun to
apply discrete geometric methods to the analysis of interbrain networks
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{hinrichs2025geometry}. Hyperscanning, the simultaneous measurement of
neural activity across multiple interacting subjects </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{montague2002hyperscanning},
traditionally relies on correlation-based synchrony metrics
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{hakim2023quantification,hamilton2021hyperscanning}. These approaches are
descriptive but provide limited mechanistic insight. By contrast, curvature-based
analyses interpret neural interactions through the evolving geometry of dynamic
graphs.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Discrete Ricci Curvatures}

Two notions of discrete Ricci curvature are particularly relevant:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Forman–Ricci curvature (FRC).}
For an edge </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>e=(i,j)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> with weight </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>w</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> connecting nodes of weight </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>z</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i,z</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, FRC
is defined as
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
F(e) = w</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>e </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>(
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{z</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i}{w</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>e} + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{z</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j}{w</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>e}
- </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sim</span></span><span> i,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\neq</span></span><span> e} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{z</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sqrt</span></span><span>{w</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>e w</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i}}}
- </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sim</span></span><span> j,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\neq</span></span><span> e} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{z</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sqrt</span></span><span>{w</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>e w</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j}}}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Positive values indicate redundant connectivity (edges embedded in dense regions);
negative values highlight bridges between modules.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Ollivier–Ricci curvature (ORC).}
Given the neighborhoods of adjacent nodes </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>u,v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, define uniform measures </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>u,m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>
over their </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-hop neighborhoods. The Wasserstein-1 distance </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>W</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1(m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>u,m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>v)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> between
these measures defines curvature as
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span>(u,v) = 1 - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{W</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1(m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>u,m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>v)}{d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>G(u,v)}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Negative curvature corresponds to bottlenecks that attract information flow;
positive curvature corresponds to diffusion-promoting regions.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Entropy of Curvature Distributions}

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{RC}(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denote the density of curvature values across the interbrain
graph </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>G</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> at time </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. The entropy of this distribution,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{RC}(G</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t) = - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span> f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{RC}(x) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\log</span></span><span> f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{RC}(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> dx,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
captures the diversity of local geometric configurations. Divergences in
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{RC}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> signal phase transitions in network topology, e.g.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span>shifts from
segregated lattice-like organization to integrated small-world connectivity
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{znaidi2023unified,muldoon2016smallworld}. These divergences align with
behavioral transitions in interaction (cooperation, rupture, repair).

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Integration with SPC and RSVP}

We propose three levels of correspondence:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{SPC adequacy diagrams.} Commutativity ensures that operational
  and denotational semantics coincide; failure corresponds to non-gluable sheaves.
  In interbrain geometry, curvature-entropy divergences mark the same failure of
  gluing across interacting neural manifolds.

  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{RSVP entropy tears.} In RSVP, entropy accumulation (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span>
  v &gt; 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>) produces redshift and structural misalignment. Analogously, negative
  curvature and entropy peaks in interbrain graphs signal rupture of negentropic
  corridors for information flow.

  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Information routing.} ORC interprets subnetworks as interpolating
  between shortest-path routing and diffusion </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{avena2019spectrum}. RSVP
  similarly models vector fields </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> as interpolating between directed transport
  (negentropic channels) and diffusion (entropic dissipation).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Sheaf-Theoretic Recasting}

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span>U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denote neural patches (regions of interest) across interacting
brains, with interbrain couplings realized as overlaps </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cap</span></span><span> U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Assign to
each </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> the local curvature distribution </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{RC}|</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. A sheaf condition
requires that local curvature assignments agree on overlaps:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{RC}|</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cap</span></span><span> U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j} = f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{RC}|</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>|</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cap</span></span><span> U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j} =
f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{RC}|</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>|</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cap</span></span><span> U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Failures of this gluing correspond to entropy divergences in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{RC}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, i.e.<span class="hljs-keyword">\
</span>phase transitions. Thus, adequacy of interbrain synchrony is equivalent to
sheaf-coherence of curvature distributions.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Broader Implications}

Curvature-entropy methods thereby unify three domains:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{enumerate}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Semantic computation} (SPC) where adequacy diagrams enforce
  categorical commutativity.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Cosmological dynamics} (RSVP) where entropy production generates
  redshift and structure.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Social neuroscience} where interbrain synchrony emerges from
  curvature-preserving network flows.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{enumerate}
All three can be understood as instances of a single principle: coherence is
preservation of curvature under projection, and rupture is its violation.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
<hr data-start="5044" data-end="5047">
<p data-start="5049" data-end="5213">This turns the interbrain geometry paper into a mathematically rigorous bridge between SPC adequacy, RSVP entropy-tears, and curvature-based network neuroscience.</p>
