The study of high-dimensional data and social neural interaction has long been
framed through geometric and topological metaphors. The </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{manifold
hypothesis} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{gorban2018blessing,fefferman2016testing,olah2014manifolds,chollet2021deep}
proposed that seemingly high-dimensional data in fact concentrate on
low-dimensional submanifolds. This observation underpinned the success of
nonlinear dimensionality reduction and deep learning, where generalization is
achieved by interpolating along continuous paths within these latent manifolds.
Information geometry provided the mathematical articulation of this view,
identifying statistical manifolds equipped with the Fisher metric as the
substrate for inference and learning </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{caticha2015geometry}. Through the
free energy principle and active inference, these manifold structures were
linked to the dynamics of perception and prediction under Markov blankets
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{kirchhoff2018markov}.

Topology entered this discourse as a method for quantifying global
structure—whether through persistent homology in machine learning, or through
sheaf-theoretic models of local-to-global consistency in semantics and logic.
These approaches emphasized that coherence requires not only local smoothness
but also global gluing conditions, a theme carried forward in recent
developments such as the Spherepop Calculus and RSVP.

In parallel, social neuroscience developed techniques to measure neural
coordination across individuals. Early hyperscanning studies employed fMRI
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{montague2002hyperscanning} and later EEG and fNIRS to quantify
inter-brain synchrony. Metrics such as the Phase Locking Value (PLV) provided
descriptive accounts of coupled oscillations </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{hakim2023quantification}, but
remained limited in explanatory scope. Recognizing this, researchers began to
incorporate tools from network science </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{sporns2010networks}, and more
recently from discrete geometry. Forman–Ricci curvature
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{forman2003bochner} and Ollivier–Ricci curvature </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{ollivier2009ricci}
were adapted to brain graphs </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{weber2019curvature,chatterjee2021detecting},
revealing structural transitions and bottlenecks in intra-brain dynamics. Hinrichs
and collaborators extended these methods to </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{interbrain networks}, proposing
geometric hyperscanning as a means to detect rupture and repair in dyadic
interaction </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{hinrichs2025hyperscanning,hinrichs2025geometry}.

Thus, two trajectories—the geometry of representation in machine learning and
the geometry of interaction in social neuroscience—are converging. Both treat
manifolds, curvature, and entropy as fundamental descriptors of how complex
systems compress, distort, and reconfigure information. This historical
convergence frames the present essay: to articulate a unified geometry of
semantic manifolds across computation, cognition, and interaction.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
<hr data-start="3160" data-end="3163">
<p data-start="3165" data-end="3198">This section sets the stage by:</p>
<ul data-start="3199" data-end="3516">
<li data-start="3199" data-end="3265">
<p data-start="3201" data-end="3265">Anchoring in the manifold hypothesis and information geometry.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3266" data-end="3345">
<p data-start="3268" data-end="3345">Showing the role of topology and sheaf theory in ensuring global coherence.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3346" data-end="3425">
<p data-start="3348" data-end="3425">Tracing hyperscanning from synchrony metrics to curvature-based approaches.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3426" data-end="3516">
<p data-start="3428" data-end="3516">Leading directly into your proposal for unifying SPC/RSVP with hyperscanning geometry.</p>
</li>
</ul>
