To situate our framework historically, we summarize key developments in logic,
geometry, and neuroscience that have progressively converged toward the
geometric analysis of semantic and interbrain manifolds.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{1940s–1960s: Foundations of computation and geometry.}
  Church introduces the </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-calculus (1940) as a formal model of
  computation </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{church1940formulation}, while early advances in topology and
  category theory establish the modern language of manifolds and functors.
  Mac Lane’s coherence theorems (1963) and Lawvere’s categorical logic (1970)
  formalize syntax–semantics correspondences.

  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{1970s–1980s: Type theory and information geometry.}
  Martin-Löf’s intuitionistic type theory (1975) and dependent types
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{martinlof1975intuitionistic} provide a logical foundation for
  constructive mathematics. Parallelly, Amari and others lay the groundwork of
  information geometry, where the Fisher information metric defines the unique
  Riemannian structure of statistical manifolds.

  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{1990s–2000s: Hyperscanning and brain networks.}
  Montague et al. (2002) introduce hyperscanning fMRI
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{montague2002hyperscanning}, inaugurating a new era of social
  neuroscience. Concurrently, Sporns (2010) emphasizes meso-scale network
  features—hubs, clusters, and bridges—as fundamental to brain function
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{sporns2010networks}.

  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{2010s: Topological data analysis and curvature methods.}
  Persistent homology and sheaf-theoretic tools become central to data analysis,
  highlighting the importance of global structure in high-dimensional data.
  Forman’s discrete Ricci curvature (2003) and Ollivier’s curvature for Markov
  chains (2009) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{forman2003bochner,ollivier2009ricci} are adapted to
  network science and brain connectivity
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{weber2019curvature,chatterjee2021detecting}.

  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{2020s: Geometric machine learning and interbrain networks.}
  Weber (2025) articulates geometric machine learning as a unifying framework
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{weber2025geometric}. Hinrichs, Guzmán, and Weber (2025) extend curvature
  methods to interbrain hyperscanning, proposing a geometry of interbrain
  networks to detect phase transitions in social interaction
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{hinrichs2025geometry,hinrichs2025hyperscanning}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Synthesis.}  
This trajectory traces the convergence of computational logic, information
geometry, and discrete curvature methods with hyperscanning neuroscience. The
historical arc shows how manifold and topological perspectives have migrated
from abstract mathematics into practical tools for probing the dynamics of
cognition, interaction, and representation.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
