2015 6.85326 16.2015 5.64676 15.5196 4.81056L15.3634 4.63673Z"></path></svg></span></button></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full focus:outline-none scroll-mt-[calc(var(--header-height)+min(200px,max(70px,20svh)))]" tabindex="-1" dir="auto" data-turn-id="42aadba3-2a52-47c1-b517-178b6310b2e6" data-testid="conversation-turn-128" data-scroll-anchor="false" data-turn="assistant"><h6 class="sr-only">ChatGPT said:</h6><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] thread-sm:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] thread-lg:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] thread-lg:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="710520f1-73a8-4cef-a627-27190f0558e3" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="223">Right — the <strong data-start="12" data-end="34">ontogenetic parade</strong> is a well-documented developmental phenomenon in psychology. It refers to the predictable emergence, stabilization (plateau), and decline of common childhood fears across age. For example:</p>
<ul data-start="225" data-end="450">
<li data-start="225" data-end="307">
<p data-start="227" data-end="307"><strong data-start="227" data-end="253">Early fears (infancy):</strong> Loud noises, strangers, separation from caregivers.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="308" data-end="379">
<p data-start="310" data-end="379"><strong data-start="310" data-end="337">Middle-childhood fears:</strong> Animals, darkness, supernatural agents.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="380" data-end="450">
<p data-start="382" data-end="450"><strong data-start="382" data-end="398">Later fears:</strong> Social embarrassment, failure, injury, mortality.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="452" data-end="663">The metaphor of a <strong data-start="470" data-end="482">“parade”</strong> captures how these fears march across the developmental timeline: new ones emerge, dominate for a period, and then fade, often replaced by more abstract or socially mediated fears.</p>
<hr data-start="665" data-end="668">
<h3 data-start="670" data-end="716">Integrating with your RSVP-based framework</h3>
<p data-start="718" data-end="811">If we fold this into your <strong data-start="744" data-end="771">learning-as-inoculation</strong> and <strong data-start="776" data-end="804">play-as-simulated danger</strong> model:</p>
<ol data-start="813" data-end="1679">
<li data-start="813" data-end="1034">
<p data-start="816" data-end="1034"><strong data-start="816" data-end="830">Emergence:</strong><br data-start="830" data-end="833">
A new fear corresponds to a <strong data-start="864" data-end="889">local curvature spike</strong> on the semantic manifold, producing high surprise <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05764em;">S</span></span></span></span>. For instance, separation at age 1–2 generates sharp curvature along the caregiver axis.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1036" data-end="1396">
<p data-start="1039" data-end="1396"><strong data-start="1039" data-end="1051">Plateau:</strong><br data-start="1051" data-end="1054">
The fear stabilizes as the manifold thickens around this ridge; the child repeatedly encounters the context (e.g., dogs, darkness), leading to <strong data-start="1200" data-end="1223">partial inoculation</strong> through experience and bounded exposure. In RSVP terms, scalar capacity <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord">Φ</span></span></span></span> expands, corridor width <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.02691em;">w</span></span></span></span> increases, and entropy <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05764em;">S</span></span></span></span> settles into a manageable range.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1398" data-end="1679">
<p data-start="1401" data-end="1679"><strong data-start="1401" data-end="1413">Decline:</strong><br data-start="1413" data-end="1416">
Through <strong data-start="1427" data-end="1435">play</strong> (safe simulated exposure) and <strong data-start="1466" data-end="1478">learning</strong> (updating priors via recurrent benign outcomes), the curvature is flattened, and the fear loses its salience. The semantic manifold now has smoother geodesics across what was once a hazardous ridge.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start="1681" data-end="1728">Mathematically, each fear follows a trajectory:</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo separator="true">,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo separator="true">,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo separator="true">,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>:</mo><mspace width="1em"></mspace><mtext>spike&nbsp;(emergence)</mtext><mtext>  </mtext><mo>→</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mtext>quasi-stable&nbsp;plateau</mtext><mtext>  </mtext><mo>→</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mtext>decay&nbsp;(decline)</mtext><mi mathvariant="normal">.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S_t,\kappa_t,w_t,\Phi_t): \quad 
\text{spike (emergence)} \;\to\; 
\text{quasi-stable plateau} \;\to\; 
\text{decay (decline)}.</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05764em;">S</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2806em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0576em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">t</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">κ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2806em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">t</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.02691em;">w</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2806em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0269em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">t</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord">Φ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2806em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">t</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">:</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 1em;"></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord text"><span class="mord">spike&nbsp;(emergence)</span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">→</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8889em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord text"><span class="mord">quasi-stable&nbsp;plateau</span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">→</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord text"><span class="mord">decay&nbsp;(decline)</span></span><span class="mord">.</span></span></span></span></span>
<hr data-start="1865" data-end="1868">
<h3 data-start="1870" data-end="1906">Why this matters for your theory</h3>
<ul data-start="1907" data-end="2627">
<li data-start="1907" data-end="2154">
<p data-start="1909" data-end="2154">The <strong data-start="1913" data-end="1935">ontogenetic parade</strong> provides <strong data-start="1945" data-end="1968">empirical grounding</strong> for your formalism: fears evolve predictably because inoculation (learning) and simulated danger (play) are universal developmental mechanisms for regulating curvature–entropy spikes.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2155" data-end="2351">
<p data-start="2157" data-end="2351">It links to your notion of <strong data-start="2184" data-end="2231">recursive self-inoculation against surprise</strong>: each developmental stage introduces novel surprises, which are then regulated through experience until smoothed out.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2352" data-end="2627">
<p data-start="2354" data-end="2627">It situates the RSVP formalism within <strong data-start="2392" data-end="2420">developmental psychiatry</strong>: failure to traverse the parade (e.g., fear not declining, or persisting into adulthood) can be modeled as failure of curvature flattening (persistent high <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">κ</span></span></span></span>) or corridor narrowing (small <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.02691em;">w</span></span></span></span>).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2629" data-end="2632">
