e-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="169">You’re right: <code data-start="14" data-end="38">marks2024developmental</code> doesn’t exist as a real reference. It looks like a placeholder you or I drafted earlier to mark the ontogenetic parade material.</p>
<p data-start="171" data-end="209">Here are some ways you can resolve it:</p>
<hr data-start="211" data-end="214">
<h3 data-start="216" data-end="284">Option 1: Replace with a real developmental psychology reference</h3>
<p data-start="285" data-end="486">For the <em data-start="293" data-end="313">ontogenetic parade</em> of childhood fears, a canonical source is James A. Marks’ classic <em data-start="380" data-end="426">Fears and Phobias: A Clinical Classification</em> (1987), and later developmental studies in the 1990s–2000s:</p>
<ul data-start="488" data-end="926">
<li data-start="488" data-end="609">
<p data-start="490" data-end="609"><strong data-start="490" data-end="514">Marks, I. M. (1987).</strong> <em data-start="515" data-end="582">Fears, phobias, and rituals: Panic, anxiety, and their disorders.</em> Oxford University Press.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="610" data-end="797">
<p data-start="612" data-end="797"><strong data-start="612" data-end="677">Muris, P., Merckelbach, H., Gadet, B., &amp; Moulaert, I. (2000).</strong> Fears, worries, and scary dreams in 4‐ to 12‐year‐old children. <em data-start="742" data-end="784">Journal of Clinical Child Psychology, 29</em>(1), 43–52.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="798" data-end="926">
<p data-start="800" data-end="926"><strong data-start="800" data-end="823">Gullone, E. (2000).</strong> The development of normal fear: A century of research. <em data-start="879" data-end="911">Clinical Psychology Review, 20</em>(4), 429–451.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="928" data-end="974">If you want a more recent systematic review:</p>
<ul data-start="975" data-end="1122">
<li data-start="975" data-end="1122">
<p data-start="977" data-end="1122"><strong data-start="977" data-end="1018">Field, A. P., &amp; Lester, K. J. (2010).</strong> Is there room for “developmental” pathways to phobia? <em data-start="1073" data-end="1109">Behaviour Research and Therapy, 48</em>(4), 297–302.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="1124" data-end="1127">
<h3 data-start="1129" data-end="1181">Option 2: Keep the concept but drop the citation</h3>
<p data-start="1182" data-end="1270">If you’re not committed to citing developmental psychology formally, you can just write:</p>
